  "captionsRowShowing": false,
  "defaultBPM": "120",
  "info": "<p>\nThis song, guitar-follows-piano-basic-blues.json, has a Standard-tuning guitar (called S6_listener) \"Listening\" to a Piano (called Piano_lead).  \n</p>\n<br>\n<p>\n\"Listening\" means that notes saved in Piano_lead's track are wired to play through S6_listener's track.  So you can see S6_listenerer \"following\" every note Piano_lead plays. The notes were chosen to be playable on both guitar and piano.\n</p>\n<br>\n<p>\nYou can add more notes to Piano_lead by chosing a note style from the palette (Named, Single, Tiny, Bend) then clicking on a piano key and these will automatically appear on the guitar.  You may have to scroll the piano left and right to be in the same pitch range on both instruments.  You can hover around until you see a scrollbar near the bottom of each instrument, or you can do a two-finger trackpad scroll.   To know which pitches are identical, use <b>View | Show MIDI Number</b>.   To get the MIDI Number to stick around as you navigate away from this Section, click <b>SAVE Display Options</b> before you leave the <b>View</b> menu.\n</p>\n<br>\n<p>\nTo get the instruments to fit in your browser, use your browser's <b>CTRL +</b> and <b>CTRL -</b> keyboard shortcuts.\n</p>\n<br>\n<p>\nTo see a song where the Piano listens to the Guitar, open \"piano-follows-guitar.json\".\n</p>\n<br>\n<p>\nTake a look at the Chart: \"Chart\" menu button &gt; \"Chart\" tab.  This lets you click on a Section and navigate to it.\n</p>\n<br>\n<p>\nYou can also toggle the \"Transport\" on an off with keyboard shortcut \"t\", and you can loop from there, or toggle looping with keyboard shortcut \"l\" ( l for loop).\n</p>",
